---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, url } from '../data/site';

const repo = 'https://github.com/Isaactzl/DigitalPortfolio/blob/main';
const source = (path: string) => `${repo}/${path}`;

const numbers = [
  { value: '0', label: 'model files: the bench is built from code' },
  { value: '149 KB', label: 'gzipped JavaScript for the whole 3D scene' },
  { value: '0', label: 'third-party scripts on load' },
  { value: '100', label: 'Lighthouse accessibility, on every page' },
];
---

<Base
  title="How this site works"
  description="How Isaac Tan's portfolio is built: a 3D workbench made in code, a strict CSP, and tests that keep it honest."
  page="none"
>
  <article class="wrap how">
    <span class="label">Colophon</span>
    <h1>How this site works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The short version: a static site, a 3D bench built entirely in code, and tests that fail the
      build if anything slips. The source is all on{' '}
      <a href={site.github + '/DigitalPortfolio'}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <ul class="numbers">
      {numbers.map((n) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{n.value}</strong>
          <span>{n.label}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <section>
      <h2>The bench is code, not models</h2>
      <p>
        Every object on the bench is made from Three.js primitives: boxes with rounded edges,
        cylinders, a tube for the cat's tail. The form on the clipboard is drawn onto a canvas when
        the page loads, and its QR code is real: scan it and it opens the case study. Each object
        has its own file, for example:
      </p>
      <ul class="files">
        <li>
          <a href={source('src/scripts/bench/objects/clipboard.ts')}>
            <code>clipboard.ts</code>
          </a>
          <span>the clipboard, form and rubber stamp</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={source('src/scripts/bench/objects/hdb.ts')}>
            <code>hdb.ts</code>
          </a>
          <span>the HDB block, its windows and the cat</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={source('src/scripts/bench/objects/lamp.ts')}>
            <code>lamp.ts</code>
          </a>
          <span>the desk lamp that switches the site to evening</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The 3D is never the only way in</h2>
      <figure class="flow" aria-label="Loading order of the home page">
        <ol>
          <li>
            <span class="label">First paint</span>HTML, styles and a still render of the bench
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="label">Works now</span>Ticket stubs are plain links to each case study
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="label">When idle</span>The 3D scene loads, one object at a time
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="label">Ready</span>Click, tap or use the arrow keys on the bench
          </li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <p>
        With JavaScript off, or on a device without WebGL, you still get the render and every link.
        The scene stops drawing when it's off screen or the tab is hidden, and it calms down if your
        system asks for reduced motion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Locked down</h2>
      <p>
        Every page has a Content Security Policy that only allows scripts, styles and fonts from
        this site. Inline styles and scripts are only allowed by their exact hash, and the only
        outside content is the YouTube player, which loads only after you press Play. The full story
        is in the <a href={url('work/hardening-this-site/')}>hardening case study</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Tested on every push</h2>
      <p>
        Unit tests cover the logic: the duplicate-booking rule, the stamp press, the cat's gaze, and
        the rules and answer checking in Mainframe Run. Browser tests open every page on a desktop
        and a phone and check for console errors, CSP violations and accessibility problems, in both
        day and lamp-on modes. Lighthouse runs too, and the build fails if a page gets slower to
        appear, shifts around while loading, or loses any accessibility points.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Made with</h2>
      <p>Astro, TypeScript and Three.js. Archivo and IBM Plex Mono, self-hosted.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .how {
    padding: clamp(32px, 6vw, 72px) 0 64px;
    max-width: 820px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(40px, 7vw, 76px);
    margin: 14px 0 16px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 20px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .numbers {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 40px 0 8px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .numbers li {
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 16px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .numbers strong {
    font-size: 34px;
    font-weight: 800;
    font-stretch: 108%;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .numbers span {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  section {
    margin-top: 44px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 28px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  section p {
    font-size: 17px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  code {
    font: 15px var(--mono);
  }
  .files {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .files li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 130px 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .flow ol {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 10px;
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .flow li {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 2px dashed var(--paper-shade);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 14px;
    font-size: 15px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .flow figure,
  figure.flow {
    margin: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .flow ol {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
